{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-84747f3d9987915a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                              GraphIDyOM: A graph-native Python reimplementation of\n2\t                                                     IDyOM for musical expectation modelling\n3\t\n4\t                                                                                     Lluc Bono Rosselló1\n5\t                                                    1 Institute for Interdisciplinary Studies on Artificial Intelligence (IRIDIA),\n6\t\n7\t                                                                    Université Libre de Bruxelles, Brussels, Belgium\n8\t                                                                            Lluc.Bono.Rossello@ulb.be\n9\t\n10\t\n11\t                                                                                             Abstract\n12\t                                                   The Information Dynamics of Music model (IDyOM) has played a central role in computational\n13\t                                               accounts of musical expectation by providing event-by-event estimates of uncertainty and surprise\n14\t                                               from symbolic musical sequences. However, its reference implementation is difficult to integrate\n15\tarXiv:2607.25787v1 [cs.SD] 28 Jul 2026\n16\t\n17\t\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t                                               with contemporary Python workflows, and its internal memory structures are not easily accessible for\n21\t                                               inspection or modification. We introduce GraphIDyOM, a graph-native Python reimplementation\n22\t                                               of IDyOM that represents long-term and short-term predictive memories as explicit graph objects\n23\t                                               while preserving the model’s variable-order, multiple-viewpoint architecture. GraphIDyOM returns\n24\t                                               event-wise information content and entropy, exposes internal memory structures for analysis and\n25\t                                               export, and supports access through a local server. We validate the implementation against the original\n26\t                                               Lisp IDyOM across single, projected, and multiple-viewpoint configurations, and benchmark its\n27\t                                               coverage and computational performance against a recent reimplementation. We then demonstrate\n28\t                                               how the explicit memory representation supports network analysis of learned memories, projection\n29\t                                               of expectation values onto musical networks, recency-sensitive memory retrieval, and interactive\n30\t                                               applications. GraphIDyOM therefore provides both a faithful and accessible reimplementation of a\n31\t                                               widely used model and a platform for studying musical expectation through memory, topology, and\n32\t                                               interaction.\n33\t\n34\t\n35\t                                         1    Introduction\n36\t                                         Musical listening unfolds under expectation. A melody is not experienced only as a sequence of events\n37\t                                         that have occurred, but also as a changing field of possible continuations shaped by prior exposure, local\n38\t                                         context, and learned musical conventions [1–3]. Computational models of musical expectation formalise\n39\t                                         this process by estimating, at each point in a sequence, a probability distribution over what may happen\n40\t                                         next. From this distribution, two information-theoretic quantities are central: entropy, which measures\n41\t                                         predictive uncertainty before an event occurs, and information content, which measures the surprise of\n42\t                                         the event that is actually observed [4, 5].\n43\t                                              The Information Dynamics of Music model (IDyOM) is one of the most influential frameworks for\n44\t                                         this kind of analysis [4, 5]. It models musical expectation from symbolic representations of musical events\n45\t                                         and relations, combining regularities learned from prior exposure with those emerging from the currently\n46\t                                         unfolding sequence. Its multiple-viewpoint and variable-order architecture allows expectations to depend\n47\t                                         on representation, context depth, stylistic exposure, and local adaptation [6, 7]. This listener-relative\n48\t                                         approach has made the model useful across cognitive neuroscience, music psychology, and empirical\n49\t                                         aesthetics. IDyOM-derived uncertainty and surprise have been related to neural responses [8–12], musical\n50\t                                         imagery [13], perceived complexity [14], liking [15–18], and sensitivity to learned musical regularities\n51\t                                         [19]. These applications depend not only on the final numerical outputs of the model, but also on the\n52\t                                         ability to select appropriate viewpoints, reproduce prediction settings, inspect the evidence supporting\n53\t                                         particular predictions, and adapt the model to specific analytical or cognitive assumptions.\n54\t\n55\t                                                                                                 1\n56\t\f     The original IDyOM Lisp implementation remains the reference system, but it can be difficult to\n57\tinstall, extend, and integrate with contemporary Python-based music-analysis workflows. Py2LispIDyOM\n58\timproves access by wrapping the Lisp system from Python, but the predictive machinery still runs inside\n59\tthe underlying Lisp environment and exposes mainly the resulting outputs [20]. IDyOMpy provides a\n60\tuseful native Python baseline, but it implements a smaller modelling surface than the reference architecture\n61\tand does not expose all model configuration choices needed for direct parity [21]. There is therefore a\n62\tgap between needs that are often required together: faithful reproduction of the reference model, ordinary\n63\tPython accessibility, and direct inspection of the internal memory structures that generate its predictions.\n64\t     Making those internal structures explicit is important not only for software accessibility and repro-\n65\tducibility, but also for addressing a broader methodological gap in how musical structure and musical\n66\texpectation are currently studied. Musical structure can be characterised independently of a listener\n67\tmodel through regularities present in the musical artifact itself. Statistical [22–25], geometrical [26, 27],\n68\tand topological [28–35] approaches have described music in terms of transition frequencies, recurrence,\n69\tlong-range organisation, tonal or harmonic structure, vocabulary growth, and relations between musical\n70\tstates. Such approaches characterise properties of the music as an organised object.\n71\t     Predictive models such as IDyOM provide a complementary, listener-relative description: the same\n72\tevent or transition is evaluated according to regularities acquired through prior exposure and the context\n73\taccumulated while listening. This distinction is particularly relevant for complexity, since structural\n74\tproperties of a piece need not coincide with the complexity attributed to it by a listener model; IDyOM-\n75\tderived entropy and information content have been used to account for perceived musical complexity\n76\t[14]. Musical organisation can therefore be described both through structure intrinsic to the artifact and\n77\tthrough the expectations that a particular model brings to it.\n78\t     These perspectives are complementary but are typically represented and analysed separately. Artifact-\n79\tcentred approaches describe the organisation of musical events and relations, whereas predictive models\n80\treturn listener-relative quantities along an unfolding sequence. Graph representations provide a common\n81\tinterface between these two levels. The transitions of a musical sequence can be represented as a network\n82\twhose topology reflects the organisation of the artifact, while the predictive memories that generate\n83\tIDyOM expectations can be represented through related graphs encoding learned context–continuation\n84\tstatistics. This common representation makes it possible to relate artifact-level organisation directly to\n85\tlistener-relative prediction: not only asking how a piece is structured or how surprising it is to a particular\n86\tmodel, but where and how learned expectations are organised over the structure through which they arise.\n87\t     The same explicit access to predictive memory also makes it possible to test alternative assumptions\n88\tabout how stored evidence remains available over time. For example, recency-sensitive PPM models\n89\tallow older observations to contribute less strongly to prediction than more recent ones [19, 36]. An\n90\tIDyOM-compatible framework in which memory weights can be modified directly therefore provides a\n91\tway to study such assumptions without replacing the surrounding prediction architecture.\n92\t     Explicit and queryable access to the predictive model also broadens how expectation can be used\n93\texperimentally and creatively. Rather than computing entropy and information content only after a\n94\tsequence has been generated, successive predictions can be queried while the sequence is being constructed.\n95\tThis allows musical stimuli to be selected or adapted according to target predictive properties, and enables\n96\tinteractive systems to use next-event probabilities as interpretable constraints or control signals during\n97\tmusical interaction.\n98\t     This paper introduces GraphIDyOM, a Python reimplementation and extension framework for IDyOM\n99\tthat represents long-term and short-term variable-order predictive memories as explicit graphs of contexts\n100\tand weighted continuations. The formulation preserves the count-based prediction machinery of the\n101\treference model while making the internal structures that support prediction directly accessible for analysis\n102\tand modification.\n103\t     We first evaluate numerical correspondence with the original Lisp implementation and benchmark\n104\tthe resulting Python framework. We then show how this graph-native representation enables network\n105\tanalysis of learned memories and expectation-annotated musical structures, recency-sensitive memory\n106\tretrieval, and external access to trained models for experimental and interactive applications. Together,\n107\t\n108\t\n109\t                                                      2\n110\t\fthese extensions position GraphIDyOM as a faithful and accessible reimplementation of IDyOM that\n111\talso provides a common representation for analysing, modifying, and interactively querying musical\n112\texpectation.\n113\t\n114\t\n115\t2     GraphIDyOM\n116\tIDyOM estimates musical expectation from statistical regularities in symbolic musical sequences [4–6].\n117\tMusical events and relations between them are encoded through viewpoints, such as chromatic pitch,\n118\tmelodic interval, duration, or inter-onset interval [6]. Predictions are informed by two sources of\n119\tstatistical memory: a long-term model (LTM), learned from a training corpus, and a short-term model\n120\t(STM), updated as the current sequence unfolds. Within each memory, Prediction by Partial Matching\n121\t(PPM) combines evidence across multiple Markov orders, allowing longer and more specific contexts to\n122\tcontribute when available while progressively shorter contexts provide fallback predictions [7].\n123\t     Functionally, the model maps the current history ℎ𝑡 onto a probability distribution 𝑝(· | ℎ𝑡 ) over\n124\tpossible continuations. Two quantities derived from this distribution are central throughout this work.\n125\tPredictive uncertainty is quantified by entropy,\n126\t                                                 ∑︁\n127\t                                          𝐻𝑡 = −    𝑝 𝑖 log2 𝑝 𝑖 ,                                   (1)\n128\t                                                    𝑖\n129\t\n130\t   while the surprise associated with the event that actually occurs is quantified by its information\n131\tcontent,\n132\t\n133\t                                       𝐼𝐶 (𝑒 𝑡 ) = − log2 𝑝(𝑒 𝑡 | ℎ𝑡 ).                                (2)\n134\t    GraphIDyOM reimplements this architecture in Python while making its predictive memories explicit\n135\tas directed graph objects. IDyOM predictions ultimately depend on counts of contexts and their observed\n136\tcontinuations. In the graph-native implementation, these structures are represented directly: for each\n137\tMarkov order, nodes encode contexts and weighted directed edges encode observed continuations. LTM\n138\tgraphs are learned from the training corpus, whereas STM graphs are constructed and updated as the\n139\ttarget sequence unfolds. The graph representation therefore does not replace the variable-order statistical\n140\tmachinery of IDyOM; it provides an explicit computational representation of the memory on which that\n141\tmachinery operates.\n142\t    The implementation preserves the principal components required for IDyOM-style prediction,\n143\tincluding direct and derived viewpoints, projection between representational spaces, multiple-viewpoint\n144\tprediction, variable-order PPM, and separate LTM and STM models. Because the graph objects used\n145\tfor prediction are directly accessible, the same memories can also be inspected, exported, analysed,\n146\tor selectively transformed. This explicit representation provides the basis for the network analyses,\n147\talternative memory mechanisms, and interactive applications introduced in Section 4.\n148\t\n149\t2.1   Prediction architecture and processing pipeline\n150\tGraphIDyOM follows the same prediction sequence as IDyOM, from the symbolic encoding of a musical\n151\tevent to a final probability distribution over possible continuations. The process begins by representing\n152\tthe musical input through one or more viewpoints, then matching the resulting symbolic history against\n153\tmemories stored at different Markov orders. Evidence is combined first across orders and then across\n154\tlong- and short-term memories to obtain the final next-event distribution.\n155\t     In IDyOM, viewpoints map musical events or relations between events onto symbolic sequences that\n156\tcan be modelled statistically [6]. The implementation preserves this distinction between the musical\n157\tsurface and the symbolic representation used for prediction. MIDI input is first parsed into an ordered\n158\tsequence of note events, after which viewpoint functions encode event attributes, relations between events,\n159\tor linked combinations of several attributes.\n160\t\n161\t\n162\t                                                        3\n163\t\f    The current implementation includes pitch- and timing-related basic viewpoints, derived representa-\n164\ttions such as interval, contour, and temporal ratios, and linked viewpoints that combine several attributes\n165\tinto a single symbolic state.\n166\t    As in IDyOM, the model distinguishes the source viewpoint in which contexts are learned from the\n167\ttarget viewpoint over which the final prediction is evaluated. When source and target are equivalent,\n168\tprediction is direct. When they differ, predictions made in the source space are projected onto the\n169\ttarget space. For example, if the current pitch is 60, probability assigned by an interval viewpoint to a\n170\tcontinuation of +2 can be projected onto candidate pitch 62.\n171\t    Multiple viewpoints can be combined in two ways. Several attributes may form a joint target, such as\n172\tpitch and duration predicted together, or several source viewpoints may contribute evidence to a common\n173\ttarget. In the latter case, their distributions are first projected into the same target space and then merged.\n174\tEntropy-weighted merging gives greater influence to more concentrated component distributions. These\n175\toperations reproduce the source–target and multiple-viewpoint logic of the reference architecture.\n176\t    Once a source-viewpoint sequence has been obtained, prediction proceeds by matching its recent\n177\thistory against contexts learned at different Markov orders. For a source sequence 𝑥1 , . . . , 𝑥𝑇 , a model of\n178\torder 𝑛 conditions prediction on the context\n179\t\n180\t                                          𝑐 𝑡(𝑛) = (𝑥 𝑡 −𝑛 , . . . , 𝑥 𝑡 −1 ).\n181\t     GraphIDyOM represents each order as a directed graph 𝐺 (𝑛) , whose nodes are 𝑛-gram context\n182\twindows and whose weighted edges record observed transitions between successive contexts. Prediction\n183\tat a particular order therefore consists of locating the node corresponding to the current context and\n184\tfollowing its outgoing edges. Each edge represents an observed continuation and points to a successor\n185\t𝑛-gram whose final element encodes the candidate next symbol; the edge weight gives the corresponding\n186\tcontinuation count. Normalising these counts produces an order-specific distribution 𝑝 𝑛 (𝑠 | ℎ𝑡 ) over\n187\tpossible next symbols.\n188\t     The resulting object is an 𝑛-gram memory graph rather than a reduction of the musical sequence to\n189\tfirst-order transitions. Higher-order graphs encode progressively longer histories, so the same current\n190\tevent may lead to different predictions depending on the sequence that preceded it. As shown in Figure 1,\n191\tthe current history is queried across these order-specific graphs. If a longer context is unavailable or does\n192\tnot account for all possible continuations, prediction falls back to shorter histories.\n193\t\n194\t\n195\t\n196\t\n197\tFigure 1: GraphIDyOM schematic. Symbolic input is queried against order-specific long-term (LTM)\n198\tand short-term (STM) memory graphs. At each order, the current context is matched to a node and\n199\toutgoing weighted edges define possible continuations. PPM combines evidence across progressively\n200\tshorter contexts within each memory, after which the LTM and STM distributions are merged to produce\n201\tthe final next-event distribution from which entropy and information content are computed.\n202\t\n203\t   The next step is to combine the evidence available at different orders. Prediction by Partial Matching\n204\t(PPM) assigns probability first from the longest available context and passes residual probability mass,\n205\t\n206\t\n207\t                                                           4\n208\t\fthrough an escape mechanism, to progressively shorter contexts [7]. In this way, specific higher-order\n209\tevidence is used when available while shorter contexts provide increasingly general fallback predictions.\n210\tIn compact form,\n211\t                                                  𝐾\n212\t                                                 ∑︁\n213\t                              𝑃PPM (𝑠 | ℎ𝑡 ) =         𝛼𝑛 𝑝 𝑛 (𝑠 | ℎ𝑡 ) + 𝛼−1 𝑝 −1 (𝑠),                   (3)\n214\t                                                 𝑛=0\n215\t     where 𝐾 is the maximum order and 𝛼𝑛 denotes the probability mass reaching each order after\n216\tsuccessive escapes. The residual term 𝛼−1 𝑝 −1 (𝑠) represents the final fallback distribution.\n217\t     The implementation supports the standard IDyOM PPM escape methods a, b, c, d, and x, together\n218\twith exclusion and update-exclusion options. These choices determine how continuation counts and escape\n219\tmass are handled across orders and are required to reproduce the distinct LTM and STM configurations of\n220\tthe reference implementation.\n221\t     This variable-order prediction process is performed separately for the long-term and short-term\n222\tmemories. LTM and STM use the same graph representation and PPM machinery but differ in how\n223\ttheir evidence is acquired. The LTM is learned from a corpus and remains fixed while a target piece is\n224\tprocessed. The STM is reset at the beginning of each target piece and updated after every observed event,\n225\tallowing the model to adapt to regularities that emerge within the current sequence. Either memory can\n226\tbe evaluated independently, or both can contribute to prediction.\n227\t     When both memories are active, the LTM and STM each produce a probability distribution over\n228\tpossible next events. These distributions are then combined into a single prediction. Rather than weighting\n229\tboth memories equally, IDyOM gives greater influence to the distribution that is more confident, that is,\n230\tthe one whose probability mass is more concentrated over a smaller set of candidates.\n231\t     This confidence is estimated from the entropy of each component distribution. For a distribution 𝑝 𝑚 ,\n232\tits entropy is normalised by the maximum entropy possible over its support,\n233\t\n234\t                                                       𝐻 ( 𝑝𝑚)\n235\t                                          𝑟𝑚 =                       ,\n236\t                                                  log2 |supp( 𝑝 𝑚 )|\n237\t    so that 𝑟 𝑚 expresses how uncertain that distribution is relative to a uniform distribution over the\n238\tsame set of candidates. Lower values therefore indicate a more concentrated, and hence more confident,\n239\tprediction. These relative entropies are converted into normalised combination weights,\n240\t\n241\t                                                 (𝑟 𝑚 + 𝛿) −𝑏\n242\t                                           𝑤𝑚 = Í             −𝑏\n243\t                                                                 ,                                        (4)\n244\t                                                  𝑗 (𝑟 𝑗 + 𝛿)\n245\t\n246\t    where 𝑏 controls how strongly differences in uncertainty affect the merge and 𝛿 prevents numerical\n247\tproblems when entropy is very small. The weights sum to one, with lower-entropy component distributions\n248\treceiving greater influence.\n249\t    For the geometric combination used in the Lisp-aligned validation (see Section 3), following Pearce\n250\tet al. [37], the component distributions are combined as\n251\t                                                 Î\n252\t                                                   𝑚 𝑝 𝑚 (𝑠)\n253\t                                                             𝑤𝑚\n254\t                                       𝑃(𝑠) = Í Î            ′ 𝑤𝑚\n255\t                                                                  ,                                  (5)\n256\t                                                𝑠′   𝑚 𝑝 𝑚 (𝑠 )\n257\t    where each candidate probability is combined across the active memories according to their confidence\n258\tweights and the result is renormalised to form a valid probability distribution.\n259\t    The resulting 𝑃(𝑠 | ℎ𝑡 ) is the final distribution over possible next events in the target viewpoint. Its\n260\tentropy quantifies uncertainty before the next event is observed, while the probability assigned to the\n261\tevent that actually occurs determines its information content.\n262\t\n263\t2.2   Implementation, interfaces, and outputs\n264\tGraphIDyOM is implemented as a Python framework centered on a core prediction model responsible for\n265\ttraining, prediction, STM updating, PPM combination, viewpoint projection, LTM/STM merging, and\n266\t\n267\t                                                          5\n268\t\fmodel persistence. Viewpoint encoding, memory transformations, graph analysis, and application-facing\n269\tinterfaces are provided as additional components. Optional extensions operate on the same underlying\n270\tmodel and memory objects and do not alter the default prediction pipeline unless explicitly enabled.\n271\t    Models can be trained, saved, loaded, and queried directly through the Python API. Command-line\n272\tand lightweight local-server interfaces provide additional access to the same prediction machinery. In\n273\tparticular, the server can keep a trained model loaded across successive requests, allowing external\n274\tapplications to submit an unfolding musical context and obtain predictions without reconstructing or\n275\tembedding the model. This interface provides the basis for the experimental and interactive applications\n276\tdiscussed in Section 4.3.\n277\t    At the event level, the framework returns the probability assigned to the observed event, information\n278\tcontent, predictive entropy, and the complete distribution over candidate continuations. Optional diagnostic\n279\ttraces expose intermediate quantities including order-specific LTM and STM predictions, projected\n280\tdistributions, merge weights, and candidate continuations. These outputs make it possible to inspect the\n281\tcontributions of different context depths, viewpoints, and memory sources.\n282\t    The underlying predictive memories remain directly accessible as graph objects and can be exported in\n283\tGraphML format with context labels and node and edge weights preserved. The exported representations\n284\tcan therefore be examined using network-analysis libraries such as NetworkX [38] or visualised using\n285\tsoftware such as Gephi [39].\n286\t\n287\t\n288\t3    Validation and benchmarking\n289\tWe evaluated the default GraphIDyOM implementation against the original Lisp IDyOM using 185\n290\tmonophonic Bach chorale melodies from the corpus used in the original IDyOM work [40], the same data\n291\tsource used in the IDyOMpy evaluation [21]. The validation used five-fold cross-validation with seed 0.\n292\tIn each fold, the LTM was trained on four fifths of the corpus and evaluated on the held-out fifth, yielding\n293\t9227 event-level comparisons with Lisp IDyOM per configuration. Both implementations used LTM and\n294\tSTM components, a maximum Markov order of 5, LTM escape method c, STM escape method x, LTM\n295\tupdate exclusion disabled, STM update exclusion enabled, and geometric entropy-weighted LTM/STM\n296\tmerging with bias 7.0 and no entropy-weight offset.\n297\t    The validation covers several forms of representation and prediction supported by the IDyOM\n298\tarchitecture. Direct configurations include chromatic pitch with octave (cpitch), note duration (dur), and\n299\tbasic inter-onset interval (bioi). Other configurations use derived viewpoints: cpint represents signed\n300\tmelodic interval, cpint-size its unsigned magnitude, contour the direction of melodic motion, and\n301\tcpcint a pitch-class interval representation. Temporal derivatives include relative BIOI (bioi-ratio)\n302\tand BIOI contour. For derived viewpoints whose source and target differ, such as interval-to-pitch\n303\tprediction, the source distribution is projected into the target space before evaluation.\n304\t    We additionally evaluated two multiple-viewpoint configurations. In the multi-joint condition, pitch\n305\tand BIOI form a compound target and are predicted together. In the multi-source condition, pitch and\n306\tinterval provide separate sources of evidence for a common pitch target. Together, these tests cover direct,\n307\tprojected, joint-target, and shared-target prediction.\n308\t    Where IDyOMpy could express an approximately comparable configuration, we also evaluated\n309\tit using use_original_PPM=True. Because IDyOMpy does not expose the same escape-method,\n310\tupdate-exclusion, target-projection, or full multiple-viewpoint controls, configurations that could not\n311\tbe matched are marked with dashes. Table 1 reports event-wise agreement with Lisp IDyOM, treated\n312\tas the reference implementation. For the two Python reimplementations, we report the mean absolute\n313\tdifference in information content (Δ𝐼𝐶, in bits) and the Pearson correlation 𝑟 with the corresponding Lisp\n314\tinformation-content trace.\n315\t    GraphIDyOM closely reproduced Lisp IDyOM under matched parser, viewpoint, PPM, update-\n316\texclusion, and LTM/STM merge settings. Across direct, projected, multi-joint, and multi-source\n317\tconfigurations, mean absolute information-content differences remained below 0.003 bits and correlations\n318\twith the Lisp traces were at least 𝑟 = .9997. This agreement therefore extends beyond direct prediction to\n319\t\n320\t\n321\t                                                     6\n322\t\fTable 1: Bach validation and benchmarking against Lisp IDyOM. Each row reports event-wise information-\n323\tcontent agreement with Lisp IDyOM. Δ𝐼𝐶 is the mean absolute IC difference in bits and 𝑟 is the Pearson\n324\tcorrelation with the Lisp IC trace. Dashes indicate configurations that the tested IDyOMpy implementation\n325\tcannot express.\n326\t\n327\t       Configuration        Type         Source vp.       Target vp. Events GraphIDyOM     IDyOMpy\n328\t                                                                            Δ𝐼𝐶      𝑟    Δ𝐼𝐶      𝑟\n329\t        Pitch+octave          Single      cpitch          cpitch      9227 0.0011 0.9997 1.4085 0.7192\n330\t        Pitch class           Single      cpitch-class    cpitch      9227 0.0010 0.9997    –      –\n331\t        BIOI(length)          Single      bioi            bioi        9227 0.0010 0.9999 0.7064 0.6066\n332\t        Duration              Single      dur             dur         9227 0.0013 0.9999    –      –\n333\t        Interval              Single      cpint           cpitch      9227 0.0008 0.9998    –      –\n334\t        Interval size         Single      cpint-size      cpitch      9227 0.0009 0.9999    –      –\n335\t        Contour               Single      contour         cpitch      9227 0.0022 0.9997    –      –\n336\t        CPCINT                Single      cpcint          cpitch      9227 0.0007 0.9998    –      –\n337\t        BIOI ratio            Single      bioi-ratio      bioi        9227 0.0018 0.9997    –      –\n338\t        BIOI contour          Single      bioi-contour    bioi        9227 0.0012 0.9999    –      –\n339\t        Pitch+length          Multi-joint cpitch+bioi     cpitch+bioi 9227 0.0021 0.9999 1.8703 0.6916\n340\t        Pitch+interval to Multi-          cpitch+cpint    cpitch      9227 0.0010 0.9998    –      –\n341\t        pitch                 source\n342\t  Note. Events report the Lisp–GraphIDyOM comparison count; IDyOMpy length-like rows use the closest aligned subset\n343\t  where required. Lisp IDyOM is the reference system, so its own Δ𝐼𝐶 is 0 and 𝑟 = 1 by definition. The multi-joint row\n344\t  evaluates a joint pitch-and-BIOI target; the multi-source row evaluates a common pitch target predicted from pitch and\n345\t  interval sources.\n346\t\n347\t\n348\tcases requiring source-to-target projection and multiple-viewpoint integration. IDyOMpy could express\n349\tonly a subset of these configurations and showed substantially larger deviations from the Lisp reference in\n350\tthe directly comparable cases.\n351\t    Figure 2 illustrates the comparison for an individual held-out chorale under direct pitch-plus-octave\n352\tprediction. The GraphIDyOM information-content and entropy traces are visually indistinguishable from\n353\tthe Lisp reference, whereas IDyOMpy shows larger deviations under the closest available configuration.\n354\t\n355\t\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tFigure 2: Example event-wise validation profiles. Information-content and entropy traces for a held-out\n359\tBach chorale (chor-072, 57 events) under direct pitch-plus-octave prediction. The Lisp reference is\n360\tplotted as a wide grey trace and GraphIDyOM as a dashed teal trace with open markers, making their\n361\tnear-complete overlap visible. GraphIDyOM was evaluated under a matched Lisp configuration, while\n362\tIDyOMpy used the closest available corresponding settings.\n363\t\n364\t\n365\t\n366\t\n367\t                                                            7\n368\t\f3.1   Computational performance\n369\tWe next compared computational performance using a restricted configuration that all three systems\n370\tcould express: direct pitch-plus-octave prediction, corresponding to cpitch in Lisp IDyOM and pitch\n371\tin IDyOMpy. The benchmark tested two forms of scaling. First, maximum Markov order was fixed at 5\n372\twhile the LTM training set increased from 50 to 100 and 150 files (2540, 5178, and 7408 training events).\n373\tSecond, the LTM was fixed at 150 files while maximum order increased from 5 to 10 and 15. The same\n374\t35 held-out files (1819 events) were used for prediction in all conditions.\n375\t     Each implementation and condition was evaluated in a fresh worker process. We measured separately\n376\t(i) parsing and LTM-construction time and (ii) held-out prediction time while the STM was updated\n377\tonline. Figure 3 reports prediction time per event rather than total batch time, providing a more direct\n378\testimate of the cost of successive prediction requests. The reference Lisp IDyOM runs were invoked\n379\tthrough the Py2LispIDyOM wrapper [20]; training and prediction were called separately so that LTM\n380\tconstruction and held-out prediction could be timed independently.\n381\t     The GraphIDyOM implementation constructed the LTM in less than one second across the training-\n382\tsize conditions and remained within a few seconds at maximum order 15. With 150 training files,\n383\taverage prediction time increased from approximately 0.8 ms per event at order 5 to 1.7 ms at order\n384\t15. These values support low-latency use in interactive symbolic-music applications, although they are\n385\tbatch-computation averages rather than hard real-time guarantees.\n386\t\n387\t\n388\t\n389\t\n390\tFigure 3: Pitch-octave computational performance benchmark. Computational profile for direct\n391\tpitch-plus-octave prediction on the Bach dataset. The left column varies the number of LTM training files\n392\twhile keeping maximum Markov order fixed at 5. The right column fixes the LTM at 150 training files\n393\tand varies maximum order. The top row shows LTM construction time; the bottom row shows average\n394\theld-out prediction time per event while the STM is updated online. All y-axes use logarithmic scales.\n395\t\n396\t    IDyOMpy showed lower LTM-construction costs but substantially higher prediction latency in this\n397\tbenchmark, increasing from approximately 44 to 77 ms per event across the order-scaling conditions.\n398\tWith use_original_PPM=True, its benchmarked prediction path reconstructs the STM from the current\n399\tsequence prefix and evaluates probabilities across the model alphabet. By contrast, the graph-native\n400\timplementation maintains the STM incrementally, querying the outgoing continuation counts of the\n401\tcurrent context at each order before updating the memory with the newly observed event. This shifts some\n402\tcost toward model construction, where explicit NetworkX nodes, edges, and attributes are materialised,\n403\twhile avoiding repeated reconstruction during sequential prediction.\n404\t    The original Lisp implementation remained faster in this minimal direct-call benchmark. The aim of\n405\tthe Python implementation is therefore not to outperform the reference system computationally, but to\n406\t\n407\t                                                   8\n408\t\fretain low-millisecond prediction within the same framework used for memory inspection, graph export,\n409\tmodel extension, and external application interfaces. These latencies are compatible with applications\n410\tthat repeatedly query the model as a symbolic musical sequence unfolds, providing a practical basis for\n411\tthe interactive and adaptive uses described in Section 4.3.\n412\t\n413\t\n414\t4     Extensions enabled by the graph-native framework\n415\tThe previous sections established GraphIDyOM as a faithful graph-based reimplementation of IDyOM. The\n416\tsame graph-native representation also makes the model’s internal memories and event-wise computations\n417\tdirectly available for network analysis, controlled modification, and external interaction. These capabilities\n418\textend the validated default predictor without replacing its underlying prediction architecture.\n419\t    We demonstrate three such extensions. First, the internal memories and event-wise outputs can\n420\tbe analysed as network objects, allowing learned topology, musical structure, and expectation-derived\n421\tquantities to be studied within a common representation. Second, the effective contribution of stored\n422\tobservations can be modified to instantiate alternative assumptions about memory retrieval, connecting\n423\tIDyOM to recency-sensitive PPM models [36]. Third, trained models can be queried through a local\n424\tserver, making next-event distributions available to external applications. These examples are intended as\n425\tmethodological demonstrations of how new analyses and modelling assumptions can be built around the\n426\tsame validated IDyOM prediction loop.\n427\t\n428\t4.1     Network analysis of musical expectation\n429\tNetwork approaches characterise musical structure through transition topology, centrality, community\n430\torganisation, entropy, and related structural measures [28, 29, 32–35]. Predictive models, by contrast,\n431\tassociate musical events with listener-relative quantities such as entropy and information content [14–17].\n432\tGraphIDyOM makes these descriptions directly comparable by exposing both predictive memory and\n433\tevent-wise expectation in graph form.\n434\t    The graph-native representation therefore shifts the question from when uncertainty or surprise occurs\n435\tto where and how it is organised over musical topology. It becomes possible to ask whether surprise is\n436\tconcentrated on central or peripheral states, whether uncertain contexts correspond to hubs or bridges, or\n437\twhether unexpected transitions occur between otherwise well-supported regions of the learned structure.\n438\t\n439\t4.1.1    Analysing the internal topology of memory\n440\tA direct use of the graph-native implementation is to analyse the topology of the predictive memories\n441\tthemselves. The LTM can be interpreted as a graph of accumulated exposure, in which nodes represent\n442\tviewpoint states or contexts and weighted edges represent learned continuations. The STM provides the\n443\tcorresponding online structure accumulated from the current piece. Across increasing Markov orders,\n444\tthese networks become progressively more context-specific and typically sparser.\n445\t    Figure 4 illustrates this representation for the pitch-plus-octave viewpoint. The LTM was trained on\n446\t150 Bach files and a held-out chorale was then processed to construct the STM. LTM and STM are shown\n447\tas two columns and Markov orders 3, 2, and 1 as rows. In the LTM layers, the orange path marks the\n448\ttrajectory of the held-out piece through the learned memory, while the STM layers show the structure\n449\taccumulated from the piece itself.\n450\t    A melody can therefore be analysed as a trajectory through learned exposure. Its path may remain\n451\twithin dense, well-supported regions of the LTM, move through peripheral contexts, or repeatedly require\n452\tfallback to shorter histories. These topological properties can be related to the event-wise entropy and\n453\tinformation content produced by the model. GraphIDyOM thus makes it possible to study predictive\n454\tuncertainty in relation to where a musical sequence lies within learned memory, rather than only through\n455\tpiece-level summaries or expectation time series.\n456\t\n457\t\n458\t\n459\t                                                      9\n460\t\fFigure 4: Example network analysis of GraphIDyOM memories. Pitch-plus-octave memory graphs\n461\tfor one held-out Bach chorale. The left column shows LTM layers trained from 150 other Bach files,\n462\tand the right column shows STM layers accumulated while processing the held-out piece. Rows show\n463\tMarkov orders 3, 2, and 1. Orange nodes and edges indicate the trajectory of the held-out piece through\n464\tthe corresponding LTM layer. All layers are plotted using deterministic force-directed layouts.\n465\t\n466\t\n467\t\n468\t\n469\t                                                  10\n470\t\f4.1.2   Results as networks of expectations\n471\tGraphIDyOM can also project event-wise prediction outputs onto the transition network of the musical\n472\tartifact itself. This converts an expectation trace from a purely temporal representation into annotations\n473\tover recurring states and transitions, allowing repeated visits to the same part of the musical topology to\n474\tbe compared directly.\n475\t     The structural network describes transitions present in the piece itself. The expectation-annotated\n476\tversion retains the same topology but associates its nodes and edges with IDyOM-derived quantities.\n477\tNode annotations can summarise predictive entropy when a state is visited, while edge annotations can\n478\tsummarise the information content of particular transitions. Variability across repeated visits can also be\n479\tanalysed, revealing whether the predictive role of a state or transition is stable or changes with higher-order\n480\tcontext, short-term adaptation, or position within the piece.\n481\t     The two descriptions need not agree. A state with several competing continuations may be structurally\n482\tuncertain within the piece yet strongly predictable for a model whose LTM contains well-supported\n483\tcontinuations from that state. Conversely, a locally simple transition may remain surprising if it is weakly\n484\tsupported by prior exposure. Expectation-annotated networks therefore expose where learned experience\n485\tstabilises, amplifies, or departs from the uncertainty implied by the musical artifact alone.\n486\t     Figure 5 shows this representation for a held-out Bach chorale. Panel A describes the piece using its\n487\town first-order pitch-plus-octave transition statistics. Panel B retains the same topology but annotates\n488\tit with average predictions from an LTM-only model trained on other Bach chorales. The LTM-only\n489\tcondition is used to isolate expectations derived from prior exposure from statistics learned online from\n490\tthe displayed piece.\n491\t\n492\t\n493\t\n494\t\n495\tFigure 5: Expectation annotations projected onto a musical network. Example pitch-plus-octave\n496\tnetwork for the Bach held-out chorale (chor-067; 89 events, 13 nodes, 42 edges). Panel A shows the\n497\tstructural transition network of the piece: node colour gives first-order structural entropy, edge colour\n498\tgives first-order structural information content, node size gives visit count, and edge width gives transition\n499\tcount. Panel B uses the same topology and layout, but colours nodes by predictive entropy and edges by\n500\tpredictive information content from an LTM-only model trained on 150 Bach files with maximum order 5.\n501\tThe LTM-only condition isolates expectations derived from prior exposure from statistics learned online\n502\tfrom the displayed piece.\n503\t\n504\t    In this example, the two representations show a clear contrast between structural and predictive\n505\tuncertainty. Several of the more central and frequently visited states have relatively high structural entropy\n506\tin Panel A, reflecting multiple possible continuations within the piece, but lower predictive entropy in\n507\t\n508\t                                                     11\n509\t\fPanel B, indicating that these continuations are more strongly constrained by the learned LTM. Peripheral\n510\tstates show the opposite tendency, appearing structurally simpler within the piece while remaining\n511\tcomparatively uncertain or surprising to the predictive model. Although this single example is illustrative\n512\trather than generalisable, it demonstrates how the same musical topology can support different notions of\n513\tuncertainty depending on whether they are derived from the artifact itself or from learned expectations.\n514\t\n515\t4.2   Recency-sensitive memory retrieval\n516\tIDyOM treats stored context–continuation observations as empirical counts whose contribution does not\n517\tdecrease with age. Harrison and colleagues extended this framework with recency-sensitive PPM, in which\n518\tolder observations contribute progressively less strongly to prediction [36]. The present implementation\n519\tincorporates this mechanism as an optional transformation of the counts retrieved from the same predictive\n520\tmemory. Conceptually, each stored observation retains the same structural support in memory but\n521\tcontributes an age-dependent weight when a prediction is made. Recent observations may retain their\n522\tfull contribution within a configurable buffer, whereas older evidence is progressively down-weighted\n523\taccording to the retrieval function. The graph topology is therefore preserved while the effective strength\n524\tof its transitions changes with recency.\n525\t     Figure 6 illustrates the effect of recency-sensitive retrieval on a held-out Bach chorale. Panels A and\n526\tB show event-wise predictions from the full LTM+STM model, while Panels C and D show the final\n527\torder-1 STM graph accumulated over the same piece. The two conditions use the same pitch-plus-octave\n528\tconfiguration and LTM training set and differ only in whether stored observations contribute through\n529\tempirical counts or deterministic recency-weighted counts. In the recency-sensitive condition, PPM-decay\n530\tis applied to both LTM and STM; the network panels visualise only the final STM, with edge widths and\n531\tcolours indicating effective retrieval weights at the final query time. Stored observations are preserved,\n532\tbut their contribution decreases with age once they leave the item buffer, giving greater weight to more\n533\trecent evidence. These settings are used only to demonstrate the representational consequences of the\n534\tmechanism.\n535\t\n536\t\n537\t\n538\t\n539\tFigure 6: Information-content, entropy, and STM topology under PPM-decay. Event-wise profiles for\n540\tthe held-out Bach chorale chor-036 (55 events). Panels A and B show information content and predictive\n541\tentropy under default empirical counts and deterministic recency-weighted retrieval. Panels C and D\n542\tshow the final order-1 STM graph under the same two conditions. The transition support is preserved,\n543\twhile edge width and colour in Panel D reflect the effective recency-weighted retrieval strengths. Both\n544\tconditions use the same parser, viewpoint, training split, maximum order, and LTM+STM configuration;\n545\tonly the optional retrieval mechanism differs.\n546\t\n547\t\n548\t\n549\t                                                    12\n550\t\f    The resulting profiles show that changing retrieval weights affects not only the event-wise entropy and\n551\tinformation content produced by the model, but also the effective structure of the predictive memory from\n552\twhich those quantities arise. Because the memory is represented explicitly as a graph, the consequences\n553\tof recency-sensitive retrieval can be inspected directly through changes in edge weights while the stored\n554\ttransition support remains fixed. This makes it possible to analyse the same memory manipulation at two\n555\tlevels: in its effect on the prediction profile and in its effect on the internal representation supporting\n556\tthose predictions.\n557\t\n558\t4.3   External access and interactive applications\n559\tGraphIDyOM can keep a trained model loaded in a local Python process and expose its predictions\n560\tthrough a lightweight JSON server. External applications can therefore send an unfolding musical\n561\thistory and receive next-event candidates and their probabilities without embedding the implementation\n562\titself. LTM-only, STM-only, and combined prediction modes are available, while more detailed entropy,\n563\tinformation-content, and order-wise traces remain accessible through the Python API.\n564\t     This access can support experimental workflows in which expectation is treated as a design variable\n565\trather than only as a post-hoc descriptor. Studies of musical expectation commonly construct sets of\n566\tmusical stimuli and subsequently characterise their uncertainty or surprise [16, 19]. With direct access to\n567\tthe next-event distribution, stimulus construction can instead proceed iteratively: candidate continuations\n568\tcan be evaluated or selected while the sequence is being generated, allowing stimuli to be steered\n569\ttoward desired levels or temporal profiles of entropy and information content. This could facilitate the\n570\tconstruction of controlled contrasts in which predictive properties are manipulated more directly while\n571\tother musical constraints are maintained.\n572\t     The same mechanism supports interactive musical applications. An external system can repeatedly\n573\tquery the model as music unfolds and use the returned probabilities as suggestions, constraints, visual\n574\tfeedback, or inputs to generation. Expectation can therefore become an active parameter of musical\n575\tinteraction rather than a quantity computed only after the musical material has been produced.\n576\t     IRIDyOM, a prototype developed alongside GraphIDyOM, demonstrates this application pathway by\n577\tintegrating the predictive model directly into Ableton Live through a Max for Live interface [41]. As\n578\tillustrated in Figure 7, the interface can expose the current predictive state during composition, including\n579\tlikely next-note candidates and the evolving information-content profile of the musical sequence. The\n580\tsame server also supports controlled variation, continuation, and expectation-guided generation. Its\n581\tintegration within a widely used digital audio workstation demonstrates that the prediction framework can\n582\toperate not only as an offline analytical tool, but as an accessible component of an established musical\n583\tproduction environment.\n584\t\n585\t\n586\t\n587\t\n588\tFigure 7: Interactive deployment of GraphIDyOM. Assistant mode of the IRIDyOM Max for Live\n589\tinterface integrated in Ableton Live. GraphIDyOM provides the predictive backend, returning likely\n590\tnext-note candidates and event-wise information-content estimates while the musical sequence is inspected\n591\tor edited. Adapted from Bono Rosselló et al. [41].\n592\t\n593\t\n594\t\n595\t\n596\t                                                    13\n597\t\f5    Discussion\n598\tGraphIDyOM closely reproduced the original Lisp IDyOM across direct, projected, and multiple-\n599\tviewpoint configurations, indicating that the core prediction mechanisms are consistently preserved under\n600\tmatched symbolic input and model settings. The model retains native Python execution while covering\n601\ta broader subset of the tested reference architecture than IDyOMpy and showing substantially lower\n602\tevent-wise prediction latency in the present benchmark [21]; Py2LispIDyOM, by contrast, provides\n603\tPython access while retaining the original Lisp backend [20]. GraphIDyOM’s contribution is therefore\n604\tnot a new predictive model, but an accessible and extensible computational basis in which the mechanisms\n605\tgenerating IDyOM expectations are directly exposed.\n606\t    The main methodological contribution is the explicit representation of predictive memory as a\n607\tset of order-specific graphs. This representation does not alter the count-based logic of IDyOM, but\n608\tchanges what can be observed and manipulated within the model. Long-term exposure, short-term\n609\tadaptation, the contexts supporting a prediction, and the trajectory followed by a target sequence can all\n610\tbe inspected within the same representation. The model is therefore no longer available only through its\n611\tfinal probabilities or information-theoretic outputs: the internal memory structures from which those\n612\toutputs arise become objects of analysis in their own right.\n613\t    The recency-sensitive memory example illustrates this advantage directly. PPM-decay preserves\n614\twhich observations have been stored while changing their effective contribution as they become older\n615\t[36]. In the graph-native implementation, the consequences of this manipulation can be observed not\n616\tonly in the resulting entropy and information-content profiles, but also within the memory representation\n617\titself through changes in effective edge weights. The same intervention can therefore be examined at\n618\ttwo linked levels: how a memory assumption changes the internal state of the model and how those\n619\tchanges propagate to prediction. This creates a common framework for testing alternative assumptions\n620\tabout retrieval, forgetting, or other transformations of learned evidence without replacing the surrounding\n621\tprediction architecture.\n622\t    The graph-based connection between musical structure and predictive output provides a complementary\n623\tmethodological opportunity. Statistical, geometrical, and network descriptions primarily characterise\n624\torganisation present in the musical artifact, whereas IDyOM estimates quantities that depend on the\n625\tregularities acquired by a particular model through prior exposure and local context. These two descriptions\n626\tmay interact without coinciding. A state that is structurally highly connected or uncertain within a piece\n627\tmay nevertheless be strongly predicted from prior exposure, while a locally simple transition may remain\n628\tunexpected for a model in which it is weakly represented. This distinction provides one route for studying\n629\thow artifact-level complexity and listener-relative or perceived complexity relate to one another [14].\n630\t    Mapping predictive entropy and information content onto recurring musical states and transitions\n631\tmakes this relation directly analysable. Two pieces, or two listener models, may have similar average\n632\tinformation-theoretic values while organising those values very differently over their topology: surprise\n633\tmay be concentrated on peripheral transitions, distributed across central states, or associated with\n634\tmovement between otherwise stable regions. Moreover, a state or transition need not have a single\n635\tpredictive role. Repeated visits can carry different values depending on higher-order context, prior\n636\texposure, and short-term adaptation, and the model retains these visit-level quantities rather than restricting\n637\tthe representation to their averages. Such analyses may be useful for work relating musical expectation to\n638\tperceived complexity, pleasure, and preference [14–18, 42], although dedicated perceptual studies would\n639\tbe required to establish specific relationships between network organisation and subjective experience.\n640\t    External access adds a third dimension by making expectation available during, rather than only\n641\tafter, the construction of musical material. In experimental work, musical stimuli are often created and\n642\tsubsequently characterised with IDyOM-derived uncertainty or surprise. A queryable predictive model\n643\tinstead allows candidate events or sequences to be evaluated while stimuli are being designed, making it\n644\tpossible to steer them toward particular levels or temporal profiles of entropy and information content.\n645\tThis could support more direct manipulation of predictive variables in studies of musical expectation,\n646\tpreference, or neural response.\n647\t\n648\t\n649\t                                                     14\n650\t\f     The same mechanism allows GraphIDyOM to participate directly in musical interaction. The\n651\tIRIDyOM prototype demonstrates this possibility by integrating the model within Ableton Live, where\n652\tpredictions can be inspected and used for expectation-guided continuation, variation, and generation\n653\t[41]. Such an interface could also provide a practical route for extending expectation modelling beyond\n654\tthe currently implemented symbolic dimensions. Additional musical attributes—for example dynamics,\n655\tinstrumentation, timbral categories, or other production-related features—could be encoded as viewpoints\n656\tand exposed alongside melodic and temporal expectations, allowing their interactions to be studied within\n657\tthe same predictive framework. Because predictions and interaction histories can be retained over time,\n658\tthe same infrastructure could also support analyses of creative processes themselves [43–45], relating\n659\tsuccessive compositional decisions to the expectations available when those decisions were made. More\n660\tbroadly, independently initialised models with different stylistic priors or memory assumptions could\n661\tprovide a basis for future work on distributed musical expectations and social creativity [46–48].\n662\t     Several limitations remain. First, GraphIDyOM does not yet reproduce the full viewpoint ecosystem\n663\tof Lisp IDyOM. The present implementation covers the pitch, interval, contour, duration, temporal,\n664\tprojection, and multiple-viewpoint configurations required for the validations reported here, but additional\n665\tmetrical, harmonic, dynamic, and specialised viewpoints remain to be added. The strong agreement\n666\tobtained across the implemented configurations suggests that extending this coverage should primarily\n667\trequire additional viewpoint encoding and projection logic rather than changes to the validated prediction\n668\tcore. Second, the current framework does not reproduce the original automated viewpoint-selection\n669\tprocedures, including hill-climbing approaches for identifying effective representations for a dataset. The\n670\tnetwork and recency-sensitive examples presented here are methodological demonstrations rather than\n671\ttests of specific perceptual or memory hypotheses. Validation and performance were also evaluated\n672\ton selected symbolic datasets and configurations. Broader testing across repertoires, richer viewpoint\n673\tcombinations, and deployed interactive environments will therefore be needed to characterise the limits of\n674\tnumerical correspondence, computational performance, and the analytical possibilities of the framework\n675\tmore fully.\n676\t     Taken together, these results suggest that the main value of GraphIDyOM lies in making the internal\n677\tstructure of musical expectation accessible without discarding the established IDyOM framework.\n678\tIDyOM provides a way to quantify expectation through probability, entropy, and information content;\n679\tGraphIDyOM adds explicit access to the learned memories from which those quantities arise and makes\n680\tthe resulting model directly queryable from external applications. This makes it possible to inspect how\n681\tprior exposure and local adaptation shape prediction, modify assumptions about memory retrieval, relate\n682\tlistener-relative expectation to the structure of the musical artifact, and use predictive quantities during\n683\texperimental or creative interaction. GraphIDyOM therefore extends IDyOM from a source of predictive\n684\toutputs into an inspectable, modifiable, and interactive framework for studying how musical expectations\n685\tare represented, updated, organised, and used.\n686\t\n687\t\n688\tCode availability\n689\tThe GraphIDyOM framework, including source code, documentation, examples, and tests, is available at\n690\thttps://github.com/llucbono/GraphIDyOM.\n691\t\n692\t\n693\tAcknowledgments\n694\tThe author acknowledges support from the ARIAC project (No. 2010235), funded by the Service public\n695\tde Wallonie (SPW Recherche).\n696\t\n697\t\n698\t\n699\t\n700\t                                                    15\n701\t\fReferences\n702\t [1] Jenny R. Saffran, Elizabeth K. Johnson, Richard N. Aslin, and Elissa L. Newport. Statistical\n703\t     learning of tone sequences by human infants and adults. Cognition, 70(1):27–52, 1999. doi:\n704\t     10.1016/S0010-0277(98)00075-4.\n705\t\n706\t [2] David Huron. Sweet Anticipation: Music and the Psychology of Expectation. MIT Press, 2006.\n707\t\n708\t [3] David Temperley. Music and Probability. MIT Press, Cambridge, MA, 2007.\n709\t\n710\t [4] Marcus T. Pearce and Geraint A. Wiggins. Auditory expectation: the information dynamics of\n711\t     music perception and cognition. Topics in Cognitive Science, 4(4):625–652, 2012.\n712\t\n713\t [5] Marcus T. Pearce. Statistical learning and probabilistic prediction in music cognition: mechanisms\n714\t     of stylistic enculturation. Annals of the New York